                <pb facs="0655" n="559" sameAs="frus:frus1921v02/pg559"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">817.00/2813</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Vice Consul in Charge at Bluefields</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Waters</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Bluefields</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1921-09-07"><hi rendition="#italic">September 7,
                        1921</hi></date>.<lb/>[Received September 15.]</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 145</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to inform the Department
                    of State that the U.S.S. <hi rendition="#italic">Asheville</hi> arrived here
                    early Sunday morning, September 3, 1921. The same day Commander J. B. Gay came
                    ashore and paid his official visit on the Governor, who reported that the
                    situation was very much improved and quiet and that the Government forces had
                    succeeded in driving the revolutionists back across the border into Honduras,
                    and he was of the opinion that all revolutionary activities had been practically
                    settled, and he did not anticipate much more trouble.</p>
                <p>From conversation with the Governor and also from good authority, there is no
                    doubt in the writer’s mind, but what the visit of the U.S.S. <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Asheville</hi> had a great deal to do towards quieting
                    the local situation, politically, and morally, etc, which Commander Gay handled
                    very tactfully.</p>
                <p>I have [etc.]</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Thos. W. Waters</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
